<p>A gunman opened fire on visitors at the Teotihuacán archaeological site north of Mexico City on Monday, killing a Canadian tourist and injuring several others before dying from a self-inflicted gunshot wound, according to Mexican authorities and multiple news outlets. Several reports said the shooting began at the Pyramid of the Moon, a heavily visited monument at one of Mexico’s most important heritage sites. The attack sent tourists scrambling and prompted an evacuation. Authorities recovered a firearm, a knife, and unused ammunition, and opened an investigation into the gunman’s motive, which remained unclear in the reporting provided. The incident has also raised fresh questions about security at a landmark destination that draws visitors from around the world, just weeks before Mexico is due to co-host the FIFA World Cup.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/Q0Dc828WgDxE78SFRIOUpIPTOFcIfke2U7af8XaRLOkzRzCV_dVAH1Ld5O1YxMuAiZdJqgzwoy-ZArU8SAmQg1tV3fjbc33OktU3icSfQt0NRKHIc_U5GxQRv6UlSEQOjWTYkSonYcoQmwfRs0heEKE5olP20453yTMcvdCGXaLmLIXxWTRd2HbPcwsPMo45hc04QJ2Vd7hc3Joe' alt='Police officers at the Pyramid of the Moon at the Teotihuacán pyramids in Mexico after a deadly shooting on Monday.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Injured visitors: Among those hurt were six Americans. The New York Times reported that two suffered gunshot wounds, while others were injured in the chaos.</li><li>Scene location: Witness videos and official accounts placed the gunman on top of the Pyramid of the Moon, firing toward the esplanade below as tourists ducked for cover.</li><li>Emergency response: Authorities evacuated the site after the shooting and recovered a handgun, a knife, and unused ammunition at the scene.</li><li>Injury counts: Reports differed on the breakdown. Some counted six gunshot injuries, while others included additional fractures, sprains, and an anxiety case for a total of at least 13 injured people.</li></ul><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>Mexico state securit